.TH ZTOP 1 "2026-11-15" "ztop 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ztop \- a system monitor for one host and your fleet, in a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ztop
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ztop
shows processes with CPU, memory and I/O meters in a terminal, and can
watch a whole fleet of hosts. On Linux and macOS it samples the local
host; the
.BR capture ,
.BR agent ,
.B hub
and
.B connect
subcommands record raw inputs, serve them over the loopback interface,
watch a whole fleet through one hub process, and drive the TUI from a
remote host.
.SS Options
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pids " " \fIPIDS\fP
Show only these process ids, comma separated.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delay " " \fISECONDS\fP
Seconds between samples; fractional values are allowed, and anything
below 0.1 is refused so the loop cannot spin.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fP
Show only this user's processes at startup.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sort " " \fIKEY\fP
Sort by this key at startup: one of
.BR pid ", " user ", " cpu ", " mem ", " res ", " virt ", " shr ", "
.BR pri ", " nice ", " time ", " start ", " core ", " ppid ", " name ", "
.BR command .
Unknown keys are refused.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tree
Start in tree view.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Render without colors, ignoring the saved theme for this run.
.TP
.B \-\-readonly
Refuse every action that changes the system: signals and renice are
disabled for this run.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print help. \fB\-\-version\fP prints the version.
.SS Keys
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc ", " Ctrl\-C
quit
.TP
.B F1
help; any key closes it
.TP
.B Z
pause/resume updates
.TP
.B Ctrl\-L
redraw the screen
.TP
.B F2
setup (theme, tree view, meter style, the I/O, pressure and sensors lines, the
header meter layout, table columns; saved)
.TP
.B F3
search (Esc/Enter close, F3 repeats)
.TP
.B F4
filter by name
.TP
.B u
filter by user
.TP
.B Tab
cycle the sort column
.TP
.B i
flip the sort direction
.TP
.BR M ", " P ", " T
sort by memory, cpu, time
.TP
.B f
follow a process
.TP
.B F5
tree view; Enter collapses a node
.TP
.BR j ", " k
move the cursor
.TP
.BR Space ", " c ", " U
tag row, tag children, untag all
.TP
.BR F9 ", " K
send a signal to the tagged rows
.TP
.BR F7 ", " F8
renice \-1 / +1
.TP
.BR I ", " A
set the I/O priority (best\-effort/realtime/idle classes and levels) or
the CPU affinity (all cores, or one core) of the tagged rows; Linux
only \- on macOS applying reports that the action is unavailable
.TP
.BR H
cycle thread visibility: show userland threads, then hide kernel
threads, then back (Linux reports thread rows; kernel threads are shown
and userland threads hidden by default)
.TP
.BR p
toggle the program path in the name column (the path shows whenever the
system reports one)
.TP
.BR e ", " l
read the environment or the open files of the tagged rows (else the
cursor's row) live from the OS into a popup: j/k scroll, Esc or q closes;
what the OS allows decides the outcome \- a same\-user process works
unprivileged, another user's process needs privileges, and the OS error
shows in the status line
.TP
.BR s
trace the tagged rows' (else the cursor's row) syscalls into a popup: one
line per completed syscall with its name and return value, the tail
followed as it arrives, j/k scrolls back, Esc or q stops the tracer and
closes; Linux only (ptrace), and only where the OS allows the attach \-
a child of ztop always works, another user's process needs privileges,
and macOS reports unavailable
.TP
.BR a ", " g ", " d ", " n ", " o ", " t
silicon, gpus, disks, network, io, thermal
.PP
The screens are also tabs: every screen except the process table shows a
tab strip above its panel with the active screen bracketed, and a click
on a label switches screens.
.PP
The mouse works too: a click selects a row, a click on a column header
sorts by it, and a click on the function bar replays that key.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/Library/Application Support/ztop/config.json
(macOS) or
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ztop/config.json
(Linux): the saved setup, theme, tree view, line toggles, meter layout
and column choices.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ztop
Monitor the local host.
.TP
.B ztop \-d 0.5 \-s cpu \-u alice
Refresh twice a second, sorted by CPU, showing one user.
.TP
.B ztop \-\-readonly
Monitor without being able to signal or renice anything.
.TP
.B ztop capture \-\-out capture
Record this host's raw inputs as a fixture for collector tests.
.TP
.B ztop agent \-\-port 7100
Serve the agent protocol on the loopback interface: a versioned
snapshot, the process list, a ring\-buffer history (an hour at the
one\-second default) and a live WebSocket stream. Loopback only until
TLS and pairing arrive.
.TP
.B ztop agent \-\-bind 0.0.0.0 \-\-tls
Bind any address instead of loopback; a bind wider than loopback is
refused without
.B \-\-tls
so no unauthenticated metrics ever leave the machine. With TLS on the
agent also advertises itself over Bonjour as
.I _ztop._tcp
under the name
.IR "ztop on <hostname>" ,
which is how the app finds fleet members; pass
.B \-\-no\-advertise
to stay silent.
.TP
.B ztop agent \-\-tls \-\-pair \-\-allow\-control
Serve the same protocol over TLS, print a pairing code, and let
tokens paired from this agent send signals and renice here. Without
.B \-\-allow\-control
an agent is read\-only to every client, however it was started;
control requests also need TLS, so a plain (non\-TLS) agent never
executes them.
.TP
.B ztop connect 127.0.0.1:7100
Drive the TUI from a remote agent or hub instead of the local host.
The same keys and screens work, including signals and renice when the
remote agent allows control and the token carries the right; a
refusal is reported in the status line like a local failure.
.TP
.B ztop connect \-\-tls \-\-pin \fIPIN\fP \-\-code \fICODE\fP 127.0.0.1:7100
Talk to a TLS agent: the pin is the
.B SHA256:\fIfingerprint\fP
the agent printed at startup, and the code is the pairing code it
printed with
.BR \-\-pair .
The first connect pairs and saves the token (and the pin) for that
host under the user's state directory; later connects reuse it, so
.B \-\-tls \-\-pin \fIPIN\fP
(or just
.B \-\-tls
) is enough afterwards. A wrong pin fails the handshake rather than
falling back, and a 401 asks to pair with
.BR \-\-code .
Without
.BR \-\-tls ,
connect stays plaintext, for loopback or an SSH tunnel.
.TP
.B ztop hub \-\-pair
Serve the fleet hub on loopback port 7101. The hub polls the agents
listed in \fIhub/agents.json\fP, files every sample in
\fIhub/history.db\fP, and answers the same protocol an agent answers,
plus \fI/v1/hosts\fP, the fleet listing with freshness and last errors,
and a
.B ?host=\fIname\fP
selector on snapshot and history so one client reads one machine or
the whole fleet. Clients pair with the hub exactly as with an agent
(\fB\-\-pair\fP prints the code); the hub's own tokens gate its reads,
while its connections to the agents are configured in \fIagents.json\fP.
A
.B \-\-bind
wider than loopback needs
.BR \-\-tls .
.TP
.B ztop connect 127.0.0.1:7101
The TUI can point at a hub instead of an agent: pick any host with the
host selector and the screens work the same.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR htop (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR renice (1)
